\subsection{Alternative self-attention mechanisms}

\subsubsection{Linformer}\label{subsec:linformer}
%\textcolor{blue}{Introduce it as a necessary mention due to cites}
The \emph{Linformer} attention mechanism was proposed in \cite{wang2020linformer} and was one of the first works to suggest a method for reducing the complexity of self-attention, which typically scales linearly in the number of tokens $N$. In particular, they introduce projection matrices $P_{\K}, P_{\V} \in \mathbb{R}^{N \times k}$ for a projected dimension $k \ll N$. The entries in these matrices are parameters which are learned during training. The projections compress the attention mechanism to
\begin{equation}
   \softmax\left(\frac{\Q (P_{\K} \K)^{\top}}{\sqrt{\dhead}}\right) (P_{\V} \V).
   \label{equ:linformer-attention}
\end{equation}
The smaller the projected dimension $k$ is, the smaller is the memory and time complexity for computing the self-attention.

